There is a sad, sad tale filled with spoilers here, read it? [ynq]

"Foo" is a 10th level Archaeologist who began the game with a *single*
stroke of good fortune:  Instead of getting the usual instant death
from drinking out of a fountain on level 1, he got a wish.  Since
Archaeologists start out with a bomber jacket for armor, I usually wish
for "blessed rustproof +2 gray dragon scale mail" when playing one.

So equipped, Foo proceeded into the Gnomish Mines.  He collected a
great bounty in armor, weapons, etc. for resale in the mine town....
only to discover the ghost of a former player, who (a) killed all but
one of the shopkeepers, watchmen and temple priests, leaving only the
candle store shopkeeper alive, and then (b) died in the candle store.

Good thing I lugged these two bags of holding I found down two flights
of stairs, eh?  I don't even get the ghost's equipment (which includes
a cursed cloak of displacement, a cursed bag of holding, $3000, ...)
unless I want to kill the remaining shopkeeper -- and I don't exactly
have the strength to do it yet (Foo is only level 7 at this point, and
has no wands to his name).

The good news is that the ghost was stockpiling armor, weapons, etc. in
the temple, so that all the things he wasn't carrying are available and
(better yet) uncursed.  I rummage through the pile for the second-best
boots, gloves, helmet, sword, etc., drop all my stuff on the altar and
emerge relatively well-equipped.

But here, at the next-to-last level of the Gnomish Mines -- here,
friends, we have a scene that will bring tears to your eyes.

I used to reserve slots Y and Z in my inventory for bags of holding.
No real reason for picking the last two inventory slots -- just a matter
of convenience.  I say "used to" because of what's about to happen.

Foo is transferring all of his excess armor and weapons into bag Y,
which he intends to leave behind on this level and grab on the way out.
Bag Z will contain unidentified potions, scrolls, rings, (NOT wands!)
and any other nonessentials that I want to keep with me.

Foo empties bag Y, fills it with armor and weapons, and then begins to
fill bag Z with sundries.  But instead of hitting <RETURN> when asked
"What kind of objects do you want to put into the bag of holding?"....


....Foo hits the 'a' key by mistake.  Instantly all of Foo's possessions
are transferred into bag Z -- wielded weapon, tinning kit, rubies, etc.,
all go into the bag.  They do this in alphabetical order, of course,
starting with item a (my blessed longsword) and ending with item Y.

Item Y.  A bag of holding.  Goes into item Z.  A bag of holding.

My *entire inventory* is *gone*.  I have to climb back up two levels
and pick the *third*-best weapon out of the pile, since I have nothing
that I wasn't wearing at the time of the explosion -- I still have the
GDSM, the rest of my armor and a ring or two, but that's it.  All the
good stuff from the first 9 levels of the Gnomish Mines is gone, along
with my very-hard-to-replace tinning kit -- and there aren't any shops
or shopkeepers to speak of in the mine town, remember?

At any rate, Foo collects the luckstone (hah!) and lumbers back to the
main dungeon without incident, where he proceeds to discover another
player ghost on level 6 (and another *looted* store -- I still haven't
found an intact store *anywhere* in the dungeon.  This one was cleaned
out by a man with a pet, from the look of things).  After dispatching
the player ghost, Foo checks the other player's inventory and finds:

A gray dragon scale mail.  What is this, a joke?

Now we come to Foo's final moments.  Keep in mind, now, that Foo is a
level 10 Archaeologist, and that Archaeologists get intrinsic searching
when they reach level 10.

Foo enters a room on the next level and encounters what *looks* like
a harmless rothe.  I've always thought that your everyday adventurer
should be able to tell the difference between a small rothe and a large
deadly mumak at a glance, but at any rate Foo retreats after the first
attack with about 1/3 of his hit points remaining.  Foo is a little
faster than the mumak -- intrinsic speed, but burdened at the moment --
so he decides to move across the room, drop a few heavy things, and then
circle the mumak and run back the way he came.

At which point my archaeologist -- my *perceptive* archaeologist --
receives this message:

"A bear trap closes on your foot!  -more-"

I now have exactly three turns to consider my untimely demise.  I have
a scroll of identify, an unidentified scroll, an unknown wand and a wand
of digging -- hey, if all else fails, I should be able to dig a hole and
escape by falling to the next level.  I start by reading the scroll of
identify, to see if the other scroll can help me (teleport, perhaps)?

No, the other scroll is a scroll of fire, which isn't really going to
do a lot of good at this point.  I try the unidentified wand, but it
doesn't do anything to the mumak -- which is now standing next to me.

My diety is pissed off, incidentally -- I'm not sure why, unless
throwing gems at a unicorn causes your diety to become angry.  Anyhow,
restoring my hit points would buy me another two turns, which isn't
really all that much of a boon right now.

So, I zap the wand of digging at the floor....


...and nothing happens.


The wand was discharged.

The post-game statistics said I was "very lucky", but I'm having a hard
time believing it.  My character just did an impersonation of Ed Rooney
from _Ferris Bueller's Day Off_, and it wasn't my day for bad karma.
What gives?

Can anybody *top* this long, sad story, or do I have the record for
the most *misfortune* (as opposed to silliest or most arbitrary death)
ever to befall a character?

